WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.939
Imagine this: you’re a researcher, with
an interest in and an aptitude for marine

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.149
biology.

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:12.180
Over the years, your work has taken you far
and wide, to the furthest corners of the Earth’s

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.180
oceans.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.010
You’ve observed countless species of sea
life, from fish and crustaceans, to whales

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:19.189
and sharks.

00:00:19.189 --> 00:00:21.240
Predators and prey alike.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.279
During one of your travels, you find yourself
off the coast of South Africa, monitoring

00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:29.039
the local population of sea creatures.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:34.250
Looking out across the water, you see a large,
dark shape moving below the surface.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.050
There is a shadow streaking its way towards
your vessel, dark as night, speeding like

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:40.120
a torpedo.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.350
You rush across the deck, leaning out over
the port side as the shape swims beneath your

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:45.350
boat.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.540
You’re certain you know what it was.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.260
After all your years of studying the world
beneath the waves, you know a whale shark

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.260
when you see one.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.460
The largest known species of fish still in
existence, the whale shark is a filter-feeder,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.850
consisting on a diet of plankton and other
tiny aquatic organisms.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.030
A whale shark poses absolutely no threat to
humans.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:09.720
Or rather, any whale shark other than this
one wouldn’t pose a threat, but how could

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.870
you know that?

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:16.090
Scanning the water, you strain your eyes,
trying to spot what you assumed was an ordinary

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.750
whale shark swimming below.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.120
But there is nothing, no sign of it anywhere.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.470
Sighing, you think you might’ve missed your
chance to see one in the wild.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.490
Perhaps it changed direction, instead of swimming
under the boat, you tell yourself.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.640
The ship turns and starts making its way back
towards the small harbor, leaving you blissfully

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.570
unaware that there’s an extra passenger
on board.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.690
As you disembark, you notice something on
the side of the boat.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:47.890
Emblazoned on the hull is a motif of a familiar
shape: a whale shark, depicted entirely in

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:49.510
painted dots.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.410
You stare at it for a moment, intrigued by
the pattern.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:57.280
It reminds you of a piece you once saw, an
Aboriginal Australian style of dot-art.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.780
You’re a little taken aback by the coincidence,
having briefly spotted a whale shark while

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.560
out at sea earlier, blissfully unaware that
this painting marks your first encounter with

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:14.030
SCP-1449, known more colloquially as the “Dream
Time Whale Shark.”

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.540
In fact, you’re so amused by seeing the
whale shark earlier, only to find one painted

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.650
here on this boat, that it distracts you.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.090
You don’t realize that the whale shark painting
hadn’t been on the side of the boat when

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.349
you’d shipped out.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:30.430
Instead, you pay the crew and thank the captain
for letting you hire the vessel for your research,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:31.930
and then head off.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.780
By the time you make it back to the nearby
town, and settle down in after a long day

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:42.080
on the water, the dotted painting of the whale
shark has already slipped from your mind.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.810
But as you begin drifting off and everything
goes dark, you’re about to find out that

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:51.100
your encounter with the Dream Time Whale Shark
is only just beginning.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.780
You’ve never been a heavy sleeper, and rarely
remember your dreams.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.151
Any that you might have seem to pass out of
your head the moment you wake up, or are too

00:03:00.151 --> 00:03:03.050
faint for you to acknowledge while you’re
sleeping.

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:04.340
But tonight is different.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.080
Tonight, you’re dreaming vividly.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.090
In this dream, water surrounds you, like it
has your whole career.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:17.209
But this time, you’re underneath the surface,
adrift in an empty ocean, nothing around you

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:19.359
but endless, blank sea.

00:03:19.359 --> 00:03:24.560
You feel something in your hand, something
smooth and moving gently like organic matter;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.470
something alive.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:31.629
You find yourself gripping the tail of a whale
shark and somehow, as is often the way with

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:37.489
dreams, you know that this is the same whale
shark from earlier that day.

00:03:37.489 --> 00:03:43.990
The one that swam up to your boat, and ended
up sticking to the side of it as a dot art

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:44.990
painting.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.560
SCP-1449 has this latent ability, while it
normally appears as a flat, two-dimensional

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.629
piece of Aboriginal Australian dot art, the
Dream Time Whale Shark can shift whenever

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:55.129
it is underwater.

00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:59.349
It still appears as a collection of painted
dots in the unmistakable shape of a whale

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:03.319
shark, but in an aquatic environment it becomes
three-dimensional.

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:11.340
And that’s what you’d seen swimming towards
the boat, not just a whale shark, but SCP-1449.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:17.380
Within your dream, you release the Dream Time
Whale Shark’s tail and begin to float upwards.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.350
Breaking the surface of the water, you breathe
lungfuls of fresh air.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:26.400
The first thing you see is land, and it’s
close by, close enough for you to swim to

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.400
the shore.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.300
Paddling through the water, you reach an island,
one in a chain of small land masses, tiny

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.540
continents in a shallow, unfamiliar ocean.

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:40.690
You look back to the water, but can find no
sign of SCP-1449.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:44.940
The Dream Time Whale Shark has brought you
here, and left you on this island, but you

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.430
can only speculate as to why.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.220
There must be a reason, you insist to yourself.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:56.020
The dreamscape around you, it would seem,
is either the creation of SCP-1449 or at the

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.090
very least a side-effect of falling asleep
close by to where you saw the Dream Time Whale

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:02.800
Shark in its painted form.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.000
Whatever this place is, there must be some
way to uncover an answer.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.090
It stands to reason, you think, that if you
can understand what’s going on, then perhaps

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:18.240
therein lies the key to escaping this dream,
and waking up back in reality.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.250
The small island you find yourself on, and
the others nearby, are inhabited by strange

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.590
life forms.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:30.020
As you walk across the shore, sand clinging
to your wet feet, you approach what you thought,

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.550
at a distance, to be a group of other people.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:35.410
Instead, you see a group of peculiar beings.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:39.380
They are not-quite human, but they’re definitely
close.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:44.151
The closer you walk, you notice these strange
humanoid shapes huddle tighter together, their

00:05:44.151 --> 00:05:45.450
backs to you.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:50.270
When you try to call out to them, asking where
you are or how you might return to reality,

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:51.680
they bustle away.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.520
The wind carries the sound of their chatter
towards you, and you’re certain you hear

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.610
them call you “another one, just another
traveler from afar.”

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:04.490
What you don’t realize, lost in this dream
environment, is that there are others like

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:05.490
it.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:09.980
Pocket worlds like this one, similar but different,
currently being experienced by any others

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.330
asleep near SCP-1449.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:17.600
As you ponder what to do on the beach, the
captain of the boat you hired is on a different

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:22.940
version of the same island, watching a herd
of multiple two-thousand kilo platypi being

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.320
shepherded by six, tattooed, three-meter tall
humanoid figures.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.710
Meanwhile, his first mate is in another version
of this d

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.810
ream world, learning how to hunt under the
tutelage of a man called Grey the Rabbit Hunter.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:41.090
Each version of SCP-1449’s dreamscape have
separate continuities to each other.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:47.061
They are differing copies of the same chain
of islands, with the same inhabitants, but

00:06:47.061 --> 00:06:50.930
each visited by a different “traveler from
afar” in their sleep.

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:55.660
Much like a lucid dream, visitors to the Dream
Time Whale Shark’s worlds can interact with

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:00.310
them, shaping and altering events in the continuity
they find themselves in.

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:04.620
Deciding to leave the beach, you elect to
make your way towards higher ground, to try

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.090
to get a wider scope of your surroundings.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:12.210
Fortunately, there is a towering, mountainous
shape nearby, standing like dark, serrated

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.569
teeth against the clear horizon.

00:07:14.569 --> 00:07:19.460
You begin your dangerous trek, ascending your
way up these dead, jagged hills.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:24.699
You’re still unclear on how you got here,
or how to escape, but you will yourself to

00:07:24.699 --> 00:07:29.590
keep climbing, knowing that there must be
some way to wake up from this bizarre dream.

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:34.250
Finally, reaching level ground, you take a
moment to catch your breath, collecting your

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.570
thoughts before advancing any further.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:42.060
You turn, gazing out over the landscape spreading
out below and all around you.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:46.599
The island is small enough you can see the
shore where you arrived, and the ocean flanking

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:49.389
this land mass on all sides.

00:07:49.389 --> 00:07:54.490
In this short moment, you appreciate the beauty
of this bizarre dream world for the first

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:55.490
time.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:08:00.180
The trees sway in the gentle breeze, moving
like the calm rolling of waves in the water

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.880
beyond, both glistening with the warm light
of the sun.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:10.199
While it is still true that you have no idea
how or why SCP-1449 brought you here, or how

00:08:10.199 --> 00:08:15.470
to leave, you think to yourself that at least
this dream environment isn’t a hostile one.

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:20.509
Not that you’d want to be stranded there
forever, but there are certainly far more

00:08:20.509 --> 00:08:23.340
unforgiving places one can dream about.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.330
A grim thought suddenly dawns on you.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.270
How long have you been here?

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:30.889
Does time pass differently in this world to
the one you’re still asleep in?

00:08:30.889 --> 00:08:34.529
A sound shakes your thoughts back to your
current here and now.

00:08:34.529 --> 00:08:39.219
It was a voice, you’re sure of it, a voice
calling out but not to you.

00:08:39.219 --> 00:08:43.839
Traversing the Dead Jagged Hills, you see
a figure, a person from the real world like

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:46.069
you, not one of the local humanoids.

00:08:46.069 --> 00:08:50.550
They don’t see you; they’re calling out
to someone in a ramshackle hut cobbled together

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.300
from pieces of wood and other scraps.

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:58.129
On the arm of the figure’s dark, military-like,
uniform is a symbol, and you can just make

00:08:58.129 --> 00:09:01.920
out three distinct letters: SCP.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.790
A wooden plank that functioned as the door
creaked open, revealing a second figure.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:12.829
The man looks disheveled, his worn clothing
patched with scraps of leather and shark skin,

00:09:12.829 --> 00:09:17.290
making him look as cobbled-together as his
makeshift hut, like he was an extension of

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:18.639
the small structure.

00:09:18.639 --> 00:09:22.220
You watch as the man talks to the agent, overhearing
his words.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:23.379
“Don’t say anything.

00:09:23.379 --> 00:09:26.160
If you say anything, I lose my mind.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:31.040
You say anything and something horrible happens,”
the strange man warns, talking too quickly

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.480
for the Foundation agent to reply.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:33.480
“You’re a dreamer.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:34.480
Like me.

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:40.949
My name is Nikolai, I am the shipseer of Dhûnhome,
and of the brotherhood of Selachiosk Pungix

00:09:40.949 --> 00:09:41.949
Combin.”

00:09:41.949 --> 00:09:47.040
The man, Nikolai, he’d called himself, seems
distressed, tripping over his words, disagreeing

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.920
with himself until he starts trailing off
into swearing obscenities.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.839
“I am not Nikolai,” he shouts.

00:09:55.839 --> 00:09:59.720
“I’m ag…agent…agent…John?” before
stumbling and cursing again.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.230
“I’m sorry…I can’t…can’t keep
the memories straight.

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:05.100
Being this lucid for this long hurts.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.579
The dreaming fills in all the gaps.

00:10:07.579 --> 00:10:10.800
Things have always been, even as they are
brought into being.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.499
I’ve been on this cliff since the beginning
of time, just like how this place has always

00:10:15.499 --> 00:10:16.499
been here.

00:10:16.499 --> 00:10:21.600
The dream was torn away by the deaths of gods
before time began but I watched it happen

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.759
five years ago and…are you following?

00:10:24.759 --> 00:10:29.259
I can barely tell the dream and reality apart
anymore.

00:10:29.259 --> 00:10:33.179
My world has always been the way it is and
we made it like that.

00:10:33.179 --> 00:10:35.970
We hurt the dreaming.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:37.209
The shark, that’s how we see it.

00:10:37.209 --> 00:10:38.209
We hurt it.

00:10:38.209 --> 00:10:43.089
Killed it in our world and the dreamtime poured
out like its spilled blood and…

00:10:43.089 --> 00:10:47.600
We made this big scar here, and…and…things
are wrong.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:53.559
Fish walk and ghosts haunt the stones and
women give birth to plastic children and the

00:10:53.559 --> 00:10:58.239
leech fields stretch out forever in the seas
of human blood and the Center eats cocaine

00:10:58.239 --> 00:11:04.660
and caviar out of panda-skull bowls on the
crushed backs of opal mares in acres of broken

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.319
glass and it has always been like this.

00:11:07.319 --> 00:11:08.689
Don’t…don’t do it.

00:11:08.689 --> 00:11:10.519
Don’t do it again.”

00:11:10.519 --> 00:11:15.259
You’re already racing back down the Dead
Jagged Hills, the sight of the deranged Nikolai

00:11:15.259 --> 00:11:17.399
burned into your mind.

00:11:17.399 --> 00:11:23.779
A man reduced to a babbling lunatic, castaway
and left alone in this strange dreamscape

00:11:23.779 --> 00:11:26.259
that SCP-1449 brought you to.

00:11:26.259 --> 00:11:31.619
But Nikolai is more than that, more than just
someone driven mad by his isolation.

00:11:31.619 --> 00:11:37.720
He is a grim prophecy of what will become
of you, unless you can find a way to leave

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.529
the Dream Time Whale Shark’s world.

00:11:40.529 --> 00:11:45.190
Already back at the beach, you drop down,
kneeling in the wet sand as the tide washes

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:46.410
over your knees.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:52.089
The how, or why, of SCP-1449 bringing you
into this dream isn’t important, all that

00:11:52.089 --> 00:11:55.279
matters to you is avoiding ending up like
Nikolai.

00:11:55.279 --> 00:12:01.339
Whatever it takes, you know one thing above
all else: you need to wake up.

00:12:01.339 --> 00:12:05.850
But whether you actually ever will is another
question entirely.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:11.899
Now check out “SCP-343 - God” and “SCP-5000
- Why?

00:12:11.899 --> 00:12:16.919
The Full Story Compilation” for more mind-bending
tales from the SCP Foundation!

